$DIR/main.p:30:1: method method cannot be an interrupt handler
    A //pu:interrupt function is entered by the processor, not called by
    Plutonium code. So it must be a plain function with a body: not a method,
    not generic, and not marked //pu:inline, since the processor needs one fixed
    address to jump to.
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2570" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:35:1: generic function generic cannot be an interrupt handler
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2570" for more)

$DIR/main.p:41:1: interrupt handler inlined cannot be //pu:inline
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2570" for more)

$DIR/main.p:46:1: //pu:interrupt function nobody must have a body
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2570" for more)

$DIR/main.p:51:1: interrupt handler noparams must take (frame *x86.InterruptFrame) or (frame *x86.InterruptFrame, code uint64)
    An interrupt handler receives what the processor pushes on the stack: a
    pointer to the interrupt frame, and for some exceptions an error code. It
    returns nothing, since it returns with iretq to the interrupted code.
    Use one of these signatures:
        //pu:interrupt
        func timer(frame *x86.InterruptFrame)

        //pu:interrupt
        func pageFault(frame *x86.InterruptFrame, code uint64)
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2571" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:54:1: interrupt handler threeparams must take (frame *x86.InterruptFrame) or (frame *x86.InterruptFrame, code uint64)
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2571" for more)

$DIR/main.p:57:1: interrupt handler result cannot have results
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2571" for more)

$DIR/main.p:60:1: interrupt handler badframe: first parameter must be a pointer to the interrupt frame, not int
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2571" for more)

$DIR/main.p:63:1: interrupt handler badcode: error code must be uint64, not string
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2571" for more)

$DIR/main.p:71:2: interrupt handler main.timer cannot be called; use x86.InterruptGate or x86.TrapGate to enter it in the interrupt table
    An interrupt handler saves every register and returns with iretq, so
    ordinary code cannot call it, and it is not an ordinary func value. Use
    x86.InterruptGate (or x86.TrapGate for a //pu:interrupt trap handler) to
    enter it in the interrupt table.
    Enter it in the table:
        idt[32] = x86.InterruptGate(timer, 0x08, 0)
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2573" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:73:7: interrupt handler main.timer cannot be used as a func value; use x86.InterruptGate or x86.TrapGate to enter it in the interrupt table
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2573" for more)

$DIR/main.p:76:6: x86.InterruptGate: the first argument must name a //pu:interrupt function
    x86.InterruptGate and x86.TrapGate build the entry of the interrupt table
    that enters a handler, and the compiler puts the handler's address in it. So
    the first argument must be the name of a handler, known when the program is
    compiled: a function marked //pu:interrupt for InterruptGate, and a function
    marked //pu:interrupt trap for TrapGate. A func value, a handler of the
    other form, a call through a variable and any other expression are errors.
    The form decides how the processor enters the handler. An interrupt gate
    clears the interrupt flag, and a handler of the plain form relies on that:
    its body is a region with interrupts disabled. A trap gate leaves the flag
    as it was, which is what a handler that can be entered again needs.
    Pass the name of a handler of the form the constructor takes:
        //pu:interrupt
        func timer(f *x86.InterruptFrame) { ... }

        idt[32] = x86.InterruptGate(timer, 0x08, 0)
    (see "pluto/arch/x86" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.8)
    (run "pluto explain E2572" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:77:7: interrupt handler main.timer cannot be used as a func value; use x86.InterruptGate or x86.TrapGate to enter it in the interrupt table
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2573" for more)

$DIR/main.p:78:6: x86.InterruptGate: the first argument must name a //pu:interrupt function
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2572" for more)

$DIR/main.p:80:6: x86.TrapGate: timer is a //pu:interrupt function, not a trap handler; install it with x86.InterruptGate
